      "title": "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "380588fd-4b25-5ff0-a1f7-7fa3cdb409c0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(4 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(4 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{4 t}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\left(4 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\Big|_{x=e^{4 t},\\,y=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(16 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(16 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)+\\left(16 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(64 t e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 16 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(32 t e^{4 t} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =16 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} & =16 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\  & =\\left(16 \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) e^{\\pi}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =16 \\left(1 + \\pi\\right) e^{\\pi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
